[{:seon.task/name "compiled-units-in-the-store"
  :seon.task/plan "Deferred, kept open (DECISIONS: Compiled units in the store: deferred): not to be released until reopened; its proof is what reopening must make green. Nothing compiles at an agent's fork (copy-on-write from the fork server, fork-ms p50 0.68 ms) or in a root boot beyond `seon.program/load-all!`'s catch-up of forms the binary lacks (the store-native paper of 2026-10-22 (git history)). A real form loads in 1.6-5 ms, ~3.6 ms of it Jolt's analysis and emit plus Chez's compile (what a stored unit would replace, by a 0.01 ms `load-compiled-from-port`) and 1-2 ms contract arming (which stays). A Chez unit is deterministic bytes, 1.7-2.6 KB for a 450 B form; Jolt's AOT units are 15x their source with absolute paths, and every Jolt or Chez pin bump strands them. Reopen when point-in-time loading is measured as the bottleneck: per-form units keyed by (form text, what it reaches, runtime fingerprint: the :seon.tx/runtime attribute every commit carries), after Jolt emits one form to a bytevector with no absolute paths. The larger cost on that path is macro fan-out: `load!` of 67 seon.lib forms with their dependents took 2,367 ms against 109 ms for the forms alone (`seon.program/dependents`)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-cost-test/loading-a-form-from-its-stored-unit-costs-its-arming}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-repl-at-a-commit"
  :seon.task/plan "A branch is rebuilt from its definitions; evaluations are history. Already true (the store-native paper of 2026-10-22 (git history)): `seon.program/load-all!`, `load-changes!` and `load-diff!` read live forms' :form/src, and nothing re-runs an evaluation. Missing: a REPL on the program as of commit C, made by forking the fork server's image, `d/commit-as-db` at C (0.7-0.9 ms) and `load-diff!` to it (1.6-5 ms per differing form). It restores code and data defs exactly (main: 1,119 code forms, 87 literal and 56 computed defs); host cells (7 atoms/delays) come back empty and host objects (ffi libraries, LMDB, sockets, procs, threads) are made fresh, as at boot; an agent's REPL state outside definitions stays with `clone!`. Waits for the owner's go."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-repl-at-a-commit-holds-that-commits-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "loading-a-changed-file-keeps-its-other-vars-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: a root started on target/seon built from src, then src/seon/program.clj edited (one defn removed, one docstring changed), with :seon.config.source/dirs [\"src\" \"test\"] (`seon.agent-test/in-agent`): in the agent (a fork of the fork server after the import), `(seon.program/graph-index ...)` answers \"Attempting to call unbound fn: #'seon.program/graph-index\", an unchanged var of the changed file, and `seon.agent/test-plan` selects nothing (four seon.agent-test tests red, one root hung in stop!). Rebuilt from the same tree, all are green. So loading a changed file's forms into a process whose binary holds the file (`seon.program/load-changes!`, `seon.fork-server/server-step` :tx, which runs every file's ns form first) leaves a var it did not change unbound. Cause to find, at its owner: the ns form's evaluation, `seon.program/remove-definition!` of the removed var, or the load of the changed form. Correct: a changed file loads its changed forms and leaves every other var as the binary holds it. Until fixed, bin/test builds target/seon before anything runs (docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/loading-a-changed-file-keeps-its-other-vars-bound}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-vars-history-is-read-at-the-repl"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the store keeps history (`:seon.config.store/keep-history?`, docs/storage.md#history) and a var's definitions are facts on its branch (:var/form, :form/source; `seon.program/live-forms` reads the live ones), but no function reads a var's past definitions. v1 had `my.program/history`. Correct: one function an agent calls with a symbol, beside `my.agent/callers` and `my.agent/callees`, answering each definition the var has had on its branch: the form's text, its transaction and time, from Datahike's history db over the var's :var/form datoms; printed by the one printer, every cut naming how to read the rest. Adds no record; replaces nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-vars-history-names-each-definition-it-had}}
 {:seon.task/name "bin-test-imports-its-schemas"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `bin/test` (test/test_runner.clj) imports src and test as program facts (`seon.source/import-tx`) but no schema resources, so schemas reach its tests' reach digests only through :env, which digests all of resources/ (docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach): a change to one schema moves every test's digest. Root's import writes them (`seon.source/schema-tx`). Correct: bin/test imports the schema resources as root does, so a schema key is a program key whose dependents' digests move (`seon.program/schema-dependents`), and resources/seon/schemas leaves :env. Measure the digests a one-schema change moves, before and after."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-changed-schema-moves-only-the-digests-reaching-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-method-key-is-decoded-once"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a method's dispatch value is encoded twice. `seon.program` stores {:dispatch (sedn/encode dispatch)}, and `seon.program/method-key` encodes [multi that-string] again. `remove-definition!` then reads the inner string back with clojure.core/read-string (program.clj:646), not `seon.edn/decode`. This is audit row C10's remnant: DUP-ROOT moved the outer decodes (program.clj:447, :705) onto seon.edn. Correct: the dispatch value is encoded once inside `method-key`, and every read goes through `seon.edn/decode`. Deletes: the inner encode and the read-string."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-removed-method-with-a-vector-dispatch-is-gone}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-form-names-its-namespace"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-24 (worktree session store, under seon.db/a-killed-root-recovers-main-in-the-durable-read-check). Measured on a store with no example program (bin/check --steps DIR with only DIR/steps = durable: no copy of the seeded store): an agent's (in-ns 'app.m0) (defn f1 ...) and (in-ns 'app.m1) (defn f2 ...), merged to main, are on main as two live forms whose :form/ns entity has no :ns/name (main holds no :ns/name datom at all), so seon.program/live-forms reads :ns nil; seon.program/load-all! on that db answers {:loaded 2 :errors ()} and afterwards (find-ns 'app.m0) is nil, and every agent after the restart answers No such namespace: app.m0. seon.program/account-tx does assert {:ns/name n} for an account's namespace, so the name is lost after it: in what the merge carries as form content (seon.merge), or in the :ns the account was given under in-ns. Not read yet. Correct: the producer that drops the namespace's name keeps it, so a merged form loads into its namespace after any restart, and a form with no namespace is an error of the load, not a quiet load. Proof: seon.program-test/a-merged-form-loads-into-its-namespace-after-a-restart (to write): a form made under in-ns on a store with no such namespace, merged, the program reloaded in a fresh process, finds the var. Replaces nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-kept-trees-program-is-its-trees-import"
  :seon.task/plan "Defect (seen 2026-10-24 on jolt/agent-conversation): a partial branch in the kept program store spreads to every tree built from it. `test-runner/program-db` keeps one branch per git tree (:tree-<id>) in :seon.config.proofs/program-store; a tree never seen is branched from the kept tree with the fewest changed program files and brought current by `seon.source/refresh-tx` over only those files, so nothing ever checks a kept branch against its tree, and a branch missing namespaces passes the loss to each tree near it. Reproduction as far as known: on jolt/agent-conversation, `bin/test --plan both DIR` at aae52f09e, 201f8f1ce and cd330af55 (cd330af55 differs from 61b4d2c9d only in notes and docs) saw 43-67 tests and named every seon.check step as defining no function, against 651 tests and 37 steps at 61b4d2c9d and 650 at main's d92b8819a; after `seon.test-store/kept-store!` removed the store (a schema change) and it was rebuilt, cd330af55 read 651/37. Seen again 2026-10-25 on jolt/agent-conversation at dedb66602 (after the merge of main f83b736e1): `bin/prove`'s plan named every seon.check step as defining no function (\"check steps naming a function the program does not define\": all of them but laws, health and lmdb-kill) and failed; the store (target/program-store-2c479bc6) had been made fresh at 08:12 that morning after an earlier deletion, during a stretch where a test file did not read (a duplicate :response key in a map literal in test/seon/render_test.clj, fixed within the hour), so a branch made from that import kept a short program and each later tree refreshed from it; deleting the store and planning again read 691 tests and 37 steps at the same tree. So a file that fails to read at import is one way a branch is made short. How the first partial branch was made is unknown: the store holding it is gone. Leads: refresh-tx re-reads a moved namespace's dependents through `seon.source/file-text`, which may answer nil for a test file; `test-runner/tree-files` given no changed paths lists and archives the whole repository (`git ls-tree -r` with no path) though it reads nothing. Correct: a kept tree's program equals the import of its tree (the same namespaces and forms), checked where the branch is made at the cost of the changed files (each namespace of the tree's file list present), never only at the next plan; a branch found short is made again from import-tx, and says so. Replaces nothing; the check is in program-db, the store's one writer. Proofs: seon.proofs-test/a-kept-trees-program-is-its-trees-import (to write: a kept branch made short, then a plan of a tree branched from it, holds every namespace of its tree), seon.proofs-test/a-kept-tree-costs-its-lookup-not-its-program (exists: the check keeps the lookup's cost)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-kept-trees-program-is-its-trees-import
                      seon.proofs-test/a-kept-tree-costs-its-lookup-not-its-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-older-branch-runs-on-a-newer-binary"
  :seon.task/plan "Found on jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-24, scripted runs dbg/near#2, #3 and dbg/fold#3 on its worktree's root): a task whose branch forked before the binary's schemas changed cannot start a run. seon.turn/work! throws seon.schema.datahike/declarations invalid-input, the registry it was given being {} (expected :seon.host/registry), and the run closes :no-end. The branch's older schema forms read as changes against the binary (seon.program/schema-changes), and the registry made from them (seon.program/schema-registry: {} when nothing changed, else contract/registry-of) reaches seon.program/schema-declarations or seon.db's declarations as {}. A fresh task forked from the current main runs. Correct: a branch older than the binary runs, its forms in effect (or its schema changes merged onto the binary's, as a merge does); schema-registry never answers a value its readers refuse. Proof to write: an agent on a branch with an older schema form starts its run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/an-older-branch-runs-on-a-newer-binary}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-contract-check-does-not-reach-every-render"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 after jolt/agent-conversation merged (1ea5bc781): bin/prove planned 29 keys of 686 tests and left 662 out as reaching a process, so a green bin/prove ran almost nothing. The edge that tipped it, seon.render.ui/did naming my.agent/prove as a quoted symbol, is fixed (jolt/a-test-reaches-what-it-calls; proof seon.render-test/a-render-reaches-no-process). The cause it exposed stays: every test reaches every render. A contracted function's form refers to the registry (seon.contract/checked, seon.contract/registry, seon.config/schema-forms), the registry holds every schema entry, and a schema entry names its renders (:seon.render/html, :seon.render/ai: seon.render.ui/agent, seon.render.ai/system and the rest), so the reach walk (seon.program/reach-graph: a schema entry's names are its neighbours) goes from any contracted call into the whole render layer. Measured at the fix: 247 tests and steps still left out, among them pure tests that start nothing (the render test that nothing is shown twice, the-screen-says-what-the-run-did, seon.context-test/each-thing-is-said-once); and any change to any render moves every test's digest, so every test reruns. Correct, at the producer of the edge: what a contract check reaches is the schema it checks (its Malli form and the entries that form names), not the renders a schema declares in its properties: a render is reached by what renders the value (seon.render's dispatch on :seon.render/html, its callers), never by a validation. The reach graph's schema entries take a schema entry's form names as neighbours and leave out its :seon.render/* property values (they are calls by name only when rendered, which the render dispatch's own form reaches). Proof: on the real src program, the tests bin/prove leaves out are exactly those whose own forms (and the test helpers they call) reach a process var by a call path that does not go through a schema's render property; a pure render test (the render test that nothing is shown twice) is selected; and a change to one render moves only the digests of tests that render through it. Replaces nothing; changes the reach walk's schema entries (seon.program/form-row's :schema for a schema entry), its one producer."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/only-a-test-that-starts-a-process-is-left-out}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-trees-program-is-the-same-however-its-store-reached-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the printer audit (docs/render.md, 2026-10-25). The plan's process tests are not a function of the tree: main at 8fbe2ec20, planned through the main checkout's kept program store (test-runner/program-db: a new tree branched from the nearest kept tree and refreshed by seon.source/refresh-tx), left out 206 tests; the same tree imported into an empty store (seon.source/import-tx) leaves out 216, the same set as jolt/printer-everywhere's. The 10: nine of seon.reconcile-test and seon.procs-test/reconciler, through ```seon.reconcile/advance```'s quoted `'seon.turn/work!` (```seon.program/quoted-vars```), which the whole import records and the refreshed store's facts for advance do not: advance's entry was analyzed in a store's history when the quoted name's namespace did not exist, and a refresh never analyzes it again. So a branch whose diff reaches nothing of it shows a jump in what is left out (240 to 250, read as red) that main would show too on a fresh store. Correct: a form's facts do not depend on the process that analyzed it (quoted-vars resolved against the program's own namespaces, the files of the tree, not find-ns), and a kept tree's facts equal a whole import's. Proof: for one tree, a refresh from an older kept tree and a whole import give the same :process set."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-refreshed-tree-and-a-whole-import-give-the-same-process-tests}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-process-tests-digest-moves-with-the-code-its-agent-runs"
  :seon.task/plan "A test that drives an agent through the server (seon.agent-test/in-agent: {:op :context}, a form evaluated in the agent) keeps its result when the code the agent runs changes, because its reach digest follows the test's call graph, which ends at the harness: the agent's code (seon.turn/observe, seon.merge/changed-vars, the server's ops table) is reached through a socket and a keyword :op, not by a call. Reproduction (2026-10-27, main fd3a7bf74f): seon.server-test/the-context-op-answers-what-a-model-is-sent was red because seon.turn/observe called a removed seon.agent/merge-readiness; fixing observe, and then seon.merge/changed-vars, left its digest (0b8d16ce8217b82c) and those of done-answers-the-judges-reasons (cf19fab0524788a6) and the new an-outside-agent-reads-its-context-after-it-defines (575b0bc26eca6ca9) unchanged, so bin/test lists all three FLAKY (\"green and red at this digest, red until a change moves it\") and ends TEST FAILED although a forced run (bin/test --all) is green. Correct: a process test's digest moves when anything the agent it drives runs does. The candidates, to be measured by reuse (the digests' owner, mid :env split): (a) the harness hands the server's entry vars over (seon.server/answer, whose ops table names seon.turn/context-block, seon.turn/hand-back!, seon.root/message! and the rest), counted as reached by the rule that counts a named var a test hands over; (b) a test that reaches a process of its own (graph-index :process?) includes the tree digest (:tree) in its key, as a reached tree-reader does, which moves on any change under src. Cost: (b) reruns every process test per change, which is what a process test is; (a) reruns the ones whose agent path the change reaches. Proof: a change to seon.turn/observe moves the digests of server-test's in-agent tests and not a pure test's."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-process-tests-digest-moves-with-the-code-its-agent-runs}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-definition-change-costs-what-it-changes"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-30 (md-scan #28's kept branch bench-28, the context session): an agent's eval allocated 47 MB at the median; the costliest ten were the platform's work on a definition change, never the agent's code: (my.schema/register! :seon.ref/block ...) 214-230 MB and ~25M ticks each (five of them, one per schema change), (seon.repl/edit! 'seon.ref/scan ...) 162-169 MB and ~21M ticks each. What a change does: records the form (seon.program/record-tx), re-arms the contracts naming a changed schema (seon.program/rearm!, seon.contract/rearm!), updates the reach graph (seon.agent's reach, seon.program/reach-update), and the turn's re-draw. A change of one definition should cost what that definition reaches, not the program's size. Plan: profile one register! and one edit! in an agent (seon.profile/measure!, touched by :self-bytes), name the costliest functions, and make each cost what the change touches (a cost proof per path, n = the program's size with one definition changed: flat). Also: those evals' records hold :fns empty, so the record cannot say where an eval's cost went: why (the record's costliest functions are taken where?), and fix it so every eval's record carries them."
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-reading-mains-history-takes-its-head"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-30: seon.task-files-test/no-shipped-task-is-merged-work reads main's merge messages (git log through clojure.java.shell/sh, whose env class is :seon.env/bin only), so its verdict was reused across the merge whose message named a task: red on main from 15b53fa3ae until a later full run, while plain proves of the next branches said it was green. What it reads is main's history, not a file of the tree, so neither the tree digest nor a file class covers it. Correct: a test reading main's history has main's head commit in its digest (a class of its own, :seon.env/main-head, from seon.fx/inputs), or the check moves to where history is made: the merge itself refuses a message naming a task whose entry it leaves (bin/prove's merge line already names the merge's tasks). Decide which with the owner's rules on checks (a check that cannot run where it judges is in the wrong place)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-test-reading-mains-history-reruns-when-main-moves}}
 {:seon.task/name "code-an-agent-test-sends-is-in-its-reach"
  :seon.task/plan "A test that runs code in a live agent passes it as text: `seon.agent-test/in-agent` takes a vector of code strings, and the agent evaluates them. A name inside a string literal is no reference (docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach), so such a test's reach digest misses the very code it tests: an edit to that code keeps the digest, and a green is reused stale, or a red and a green at one digest read as flaky. Seen 2026-10-31 by the context session on jolt/eval-path: seon.repl-test/a-broken-parser-is-named-in-a-missing-names-answer went red on the old src and green on the new at one digest (fixed in the test by building its code from quoted symbols); seon.repl-test/a-missing-name-says-the-nearest and seon.repl-test/a-broken-parser-leaves-the-repl-answering name my.agent/no-var and seon.repl/eval-string only inside strings, and every in-agent test may share the gap. The mechanism to reuse is the one for resolver calls (`seon.program/resolved`: a literal name passed to `resolve` is a reference): a string literal passed to a function that evaluates it as code (in-agent's forms, and any other code-runner named once as data the way `seon.program/tree-readers` is) is read by the reader, and the qualified symbols of its forms are the test's refs. Correct: an edit to a var named only inside an in-agent string moves that test's digest. First count the sites (tests calling in-agent, and how many name their target only in a string), and measure what the change does to the median reach; prefer this over rewriting every test to quoted forms, which the reach also reads as data, not references."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-var-named-in-code-an-agent-test-sends-is-in-its-reach}}
 {:seon.task/name "another-trees-contract-is-its-own"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-01: seon.program/account reads a definition's contract (contract-text) and kind (def-kind) from the live var first when it is bound, so a file analyzed without running (seon.source/accounts, :before nil: another tree's program, an old commit's, root's import of main) records this image's contract and kind for any var the image also defines, not its text's. The docstring had the same cause and now reads the form first (seon.program-test/a-file-analyzed-keeps-its-own-docstring). Why the contract is not changed the same way yet: contract-text prefers the live var's schema when it is plain data (a schema var a form names, resolved to its value), and the form's text alone would store the name unresolved, which moves the stored contract of every such var and every digest reaching it. Correct: for an analysis without running, the contract is resolved against the analyzed program (the tree's own schema var, or the db's), never the image; def-kind takes the compiler's account. Proof: a file analyzed beside an image whose var has another contract records the file's. The bug-class audit (2026-11-03): the class is the image read as an input nobody passed; when :before is nil, account does not call find-var at all, and the image is passed in only when the form ran, so a fourth fact cannot regress the same way; account answers a declared map, not [:maybe :map]."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-file-analyzed-keeps-its-own-contract}}]
